# PicoDrive's machine state lives in static arrays, not on the heap -- the Pico
# struct, the Sega CD subsystem's state, and cz80's two 128KB flag tables among
# them, ~940KB of .bss in total. Internal SRAM has ~150KB free, so the link fails
# with "--enable-non-contiguous-regions discards section", which is esp-idf's way
# of reporting that it ran out of internal RAM (BRINGUP 0.3-2).
#
# CONFIG_SPIRAM_ALLOW_BSS_SEG_EXTERNAL_MEMORY alone does not fix it: it only makes
# EXT_RAM_BSS_ATTR work on individually marked symbols, and marking a thousand
# upstream statics is not a change worth carrying. Mapping the archive is.
#
# The cost is real -- this state is now behind 80MHz PSRAM rather than SRAM -- and
# it is the price of the core existing at all here. Where it hurts is a question
# for a profile, not for a guess: the GBA core won 3.6% by moving its hottest
# 112KB back, and the SNES won nothing doing the same, so it is measured per core.
# The scheme is defined here rather than reused, deliberately. esp-idf does ship
# one called extram_bss -- but it lives in esp_wifi/linker.lf, and this app builds
# with RG_ENABLE_NETWORKING=0, so that fragment is not in ldgen's input list and
# the name resolves to nothing. Referencing it produced no mapping and no
# complaint: the link kept failing with the same byte count as before, which is
# what a silently-ignored placement looks like.
[scheme:picodrive_extram]
entries:
    bss -> extern_ram
    common -> extern_ram

[mapping:picodrive_bss]
archive: libpicodrive.a
entries:
    * (picodrive_extram)
